1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,000
 Hey, this is Brian from Fullstack Egg.

2
00:00:04,000 --> 00:00:09,760
 In this video, we are installing Paperless AI on a Raspberry Pi, so you can run Paperless

3
00:00:09,760 --> 00:00:20,140
 N GX, Semantic Search, and optional MCP access on hardware in your own house or shop.

4
00:00:20,140 --> 00:00:25,200
 A Raspberry Pi is not the fastest server in the world, but for a small document archive,

5
00:00:25,200 --> 00:00:27,000
 it can be a really nice fit.

6
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,680
 It's quiet, cheap to run, and it's local by default.

7
00:00:31,680 --> 00:00:35,880
 We'll start on the computer we're using to flash our SD card.

8
00:00:35,880 --> 00:00:41,920
 We have Raspberry Pi Imager Open, so we'll choose our model, and then we'll choose Raspberry

9
00:00:41,920 --> 00:00:46,000
 Pi OS Lite 64-bit.

10
00:00:46,000 --> 00:00:50,840
 I'm using Lite because this Pi is going to act like a little server.

11
00:00:50,840 --> 00:00:55,920
 We don't need a desktop environment running on it all day.

12
00:00:55,920 --> 00:00:58,680
 Next we'll choose our storage device.

13
00:00:58,680 --> 00:01:05,000
 We've got an SD card here, which is fine, but for a real archive, I would strongly

14
00:01:05,000 --> 00:01:13,440
 prefer a USB SSD drive because Paperless, Postgres, and the OCR work are going to write

15
00:01:13,440 --> 00:01:16,800
 to the disk over time.

16
00:01:16,800 --> 00:01:24,160
 We'll set the host name to Paperless, and that'll give us a friendly address of paperless.local

17
00:01:24,160 --> 00:01:27,320
 on many home networks.

18
00:01:27,320 --> 00:01:33,960
 We'll set our time zone, create the user account, and we'll add our Wi-Fi details if

19
00:01:33,960 --> 00:01:37,560
 you're not using Ethernet.

20
00:01:37,560 --> 00:01:40,760
 The important setting here is SSH.

21
00:01:40,760 --> 00:01:46,640
 We want to enable SSH so we can log into the Raspberry Pi from our computer instead of

22
00:01:46,640 --> 00:01:50,440
 plugging in a monitor and keyboard.

23
00:01:50,440 --> 00:01:54,440
 We'll add our SSH key to authenticate with the Raspberry Pi.

24
00:01:54,440 --> 00:02:00,960
 This is going to be more secure and more convenient than using password authentication.

25
00:02:00,960 --> 00:02:06,980
 I'm going to leave Raspberry Pi Connect off here because this install is meant to be managed

26
00:02:06,980 --> 00:02:11,840
 over SSH on the local network.

27
00:02:11,840 --> 00:02:13,560
 Now we'll write the image.

28
00:02:13,560 --> 00:02:18,520
 This erases the card or drive, so make sure the selected storage is the one you actually

29
00:02:18,520 --> 00:02:20,840
 mean to flash.

30
00:02:20,840 --> 00:02:25,360
 The Raspberry Pi imager will write and verify the image.

31
00:02:25,360 --> 00:02:30,800
 In real time, this takes a few minutes, so we'll edit this so we can skip to the completed

32
00:02:30,800 --> 00:02:33,000
 card.

33
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:38,840
 After this, we'll put the SSD card into the Raspberry Pi, connect Ethernet if you have

34
00:02:38,840 --> 00:02:42,080
 it, and power it on.

35
00:02:42,080 --> 00:02:47,040
 Once the Raspberry Pi has booted, we'll open the terminal and SSH into it.

36
00:02:47,040 --> 00:02:53,180
 I'm using SSH Brian at paperless.local to connect.

37
00:02:53,180 --> 00:02:59,000
 If paperless.local doesn't resolve on your network, look into your router for the Raspberry

38
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:04,880
 Pi's IP address and use that to SSH instead.

39
00:03:04,880 --> 00:03:09,160
 Since this is the first time connecting to the Raspberry Pi, we'll have to accept the

40
00:03:09,160 --> 00:03:15,960
 fingerprint, and now we're connected to the Raspberry Pi.

41
00:03:15,960 --> 00:03:22,120
 Now I'm going to copy the install command from paperless.fullstack.ag, and I'm going

42
00:03:22,120 --> 00:03:27,580
 to paste it in and run it to start the installer.

43
00:03:27,580 --> 00:03:35,400
 The installer checks the system, verifies Docker, and then walks through the paperless AI setup.

44
00:03:35,400 --> 00:03:39,920
 If Docker has to be installed fresh, the script may ask you to log out and log back in again

45
00:03:39,920 --> 00:03:42,600
 so you can run Docker commands.

46
00:03:42,600 --> 00:03:52,080
 This is normal, so go ahead and do that and then run the same install command from paperless.fullstack.ag.

47
00:03:52,080 --> 00:03:55,920
 And now we'll continue to the setup questions.

48
00:03:55,920 --> 00:04:01,920
 We'll set the paperless admin username and password, we'll pick our time zone, choose

49
00:04:01,920 --> 00:04:09,400
 the install directory, and for a home network install, we can skip the public domain name.

50
00:04:09,400 --> 00:04:13,720
 And now the setup script is going to pull the Docker images down.

51
00:04:13,720 --> 00:04:18,160
 This will take a little while, so we'll fast forward that.

52
00:04:18,160 --> 00:04:24,320
 And when this finishes, the installer is going to print the local paperless URL, the search

53
00:04:24,320 --> 00:04:32,040
 URL, the URL for the MCP instructions, and the commands you would use later for updates,

54
00:04:32,040 --> 00:04:35,320
 backups, and logs.

55
00:04:35,320 --> 00:04:41,640
 Now we'll open paperless.local in our browser, or use the Raspberry Pi's IP address if that's

56
00:04:41,640 --> 00:04:48,280
 what works on your network, and we'll log in with the admin account we just created.

57
00:04:48,280 --> 00:04:52,520
 I've got a whole bunch of documents here that I'm going to drop right into paperless.

58
00:04:52,520 --> 00:04:59,480
 I'm going to fast forward this, but paperless is going to take in these files, run OCR against

59
00:04:59,480 --> 00:05:05,640
 it, and it's going to start indexing the text so the paperless AI search can find documents

60
00:05:05,640 --> 00:05:10,280
 by meaning, not just exact keywords.

61
00:05:10,280 --> 00:05:16,040
 Now that my documents are loaded into paperless, I'm going to search for fertilizer recommendations

62
00:05:16,040 --> 00:05:23,860
 using the paperless NGX search, and it doesn't return any results.

63
00:05:23,860 --> 00:05:30,120
 And if we use the paperless AI smart search add-on, we will actually find some results,

64
00:05:30,120 --> 00:05:34,120
 including a nutrient management plan.

65
00:05:34,120 --> 00:05:36,480
 And that's the whole point of paperless AI.

66
00:05:36,480 --> 00:05:42,280
 You don't have to remember the exact title or wording from the PDF, and paperless NGX

67
00:05:42,280 --> 00:05:47,640
 stays stock, and this companion layer just adds the smarter search experience beside

68
00:05:47,640 --> 00:05:49,640
 it.

69
00:05:49,640 --> 00:05:55,720
 And now that we're on the paperless AI search page, you can see this MCP button, which will

70
00:05:55,720 --> 00:06:00,000
 open up the setup page for AI apps.

71
00:06:00,000 --> 00:06:08,600
 This MCP page has instructions for connecting paperless AI to cloud, codecs, VS code with

72
00:06:08,600 --> 00:06:17,680
 copilot, and even llama.cpp, which can be used for running local AI models.

73
00:06:17,680 --> 00:06:25,880
 The MCP token is for read-only search access, so AI tools can ask questions about the archive

74
00:06:25,880 --> 00:06:30,520
 without direct database access.

75
00:06:30,520 --> 00:06:33,320
 And that's it for the Raspberry Pi install.

76
00:06:33,320 --> 00:06:41,440
 We flash the Raspberry Pi OS Lite, enable SSH, run the paperless AI installer, log into

77
00:06:41,440 --> 00:06:47,480
 paperless on your local network, and start using the paperless AI smart search.

78
00:06:47,480 --> 00:06:48,600
 Thanks again for watching.

